<article class="post">
<header class="post-header">
<h1>Fixing slow pages, starting with the biggest win</h1>
<p class="dek">Slow pages lose readers before they read a word. Where the time usually goes, and what to fix first.</p>
<div class="post-meta">
<address>
<ul class="people" role="list" aria-label="Written by">
<li class="person"><span><span class="role-badge">Technical SEO editor</span> <a rel="author" href="/blog/authors/lena-vogt/">Lena Vogt</a></span></li>
</ul>
</address>
<p class="post-dates"><time datetime="2026-10-05">5 October 2026</time> <span class="reading-time"><time datetime="PT2M">2 min read</time></span></p>
</div>
<figure class="image">
<picture>
<source type="image/webp" srcset="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/320.webp 320w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/480.webp 480w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/640.webp 640w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.webp 800w" sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 48rem, 100vw">
<img src="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.png" srcset="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/320.png 320w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/480.png 480w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/640.png 640w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.png 800w" sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 48rem, 100vw" width="800" height="450" alt="A bar chart of page weight by part" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
</picture>
<figcaption>Images weigh the most.</figcaption>
</figure>
</header>
<aside class="author-card" aria-label="Written by">
<div class="person-card">
<div>
<p class="person-name"><a href="/blog/authors/lena-vogt/">Lena Vogt</a></p>
<p class="person-role">Technical SEO editor</p>
<details class="author-fold">
<summary>About the author</summary>
<p>Lena looks after the technical side: sitemaps, page speed and everything a crawler sees before a reader does. She writes in English and German.</p>

</details>
</div>
</div>
</aside>
<div class="post-body">
<p>Readers leave pages that load slowly, and search engines notice. The good news: most slow pages are slow for the same few reasons.</p>
<h2 id="measure-first">Measure first <a class="heading-link" href="#measure-first" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Run the page through a speed test on a phone setting, not a desktop one. Write down the largest contentful paint, which is the time until the main content is visible. Under one second is great. Over two and a half seconds needs work.</p>
<h2 id="the-usual-causes-biggest-first">The usual causes, biggest first <a class="heading-link" href="#the-usual-causes-biggest-first" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<figure>
<a class="enlarge" href="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.png"><picture>
<source type="image/webp" srcset="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/320.webp 320w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/480.webp 480w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/640.webp 640w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.webp 800w" sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 48rem, 100vw">
<img src="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.png" srcset="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/320.png 320w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/480.png 480w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/640.png 640w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.png 800w" sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 48rem, 100vw" width="800" height="450" alt="Bar chart of a slow page&#39;s bytes: images take the most, then scripts, fonts, styles and HTML" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
</picture></a>
<figcaption>
<p>Where the bytes of a typical slow page go.</p>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<ol>
<li><strong>Images that are too large.</strong> A photo saved at 4,000 pixels wide for a slot 800 pixels wide wastes most of its bytes. Serve the size the page needs, in a modern format.</li>
<li><strong>Scripts the reader does not need.</strong> Every chat widget and tracking tag delays the page. Remove the ones nobody reads the data from.</li>
<li><strong>Fonts that block the text.</strong> Show the text in a system font first, and swap in the web font when it arrives.</li>
<li><strong>Layout that jumps.</strong> Give every image a width and height, so the text does not move while the page loads.</li>
</ol>
<p>Fix the first cause on your slowest page, measure again, and move on. Faster pages also get crawled more often, which helps <a href="/blog/what-a-sitemap-does/">your sitemap</a> do its job.</p>
<section class="faq">
<details open>
<summary>How fast should a page load?</summary>
<p>Aim for the main content to show within 2.5 seconds on a phone.</p>
</details>
<details open>
<summary>Which page should I fix first?</summary>
<p>The slowest page that brings in the most readers.</p>
</details>
</section>

</div>
